{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking policy of reducing penalties for small amounts of controlled substances concludes this weekend, as a revised legal approach reintroduces criminal charges for such possession. Under the new legislation, individuals found with drugs like fentanyl, heroin, or meth will face either prosecution or an alternative pathway involving mandated addiction support and behavioral health initiatives to steer clear of fines. While personal possession remains classified as a minor offense, subject to potential incarceration for up to half a year, the law also introduces stricter enforcement in areas such as public spaces and escalates penalties for distribution near locations like recreational areas.\n\nProponents argue the updated law permits regions to tailor their own diversion programs, contingent on available funding, though critics warn this could lead to inconsistent enforcement across jurisdictions. Local officials, including Portland’s police leadership, emphasize the narrow eligibility for alternative resolutions, excluding those with prior offenses, active warrants, or histories of violence, among other restrictions. Meeting these criteria allows officers to redirect cases to county-run outreach services, where mobile teams must engage within 30 minutes—or risk arrest for the individual.\n\nFailure to arrange immediate behavioral health intervention results in detention, as law enforcement cannot indefinitely delay proceedings while awaiting support. Police emphasize the urgency of response times, framing the process as a structured alternative to traditional prosecution rather than an open-ended diversion. The shift reflects broader tensions between public safety goals and the complexities of addressing addiction through criminal justice fr